.TH MOUNT-WINDOWS 8 "September 2026" "qcom-ath-board-update" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
mount-windows \- mount a Windows system volume read-only
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mount-windows
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mount-windows
finds the Windows system volume on local block devices, unlocks it with
.BR dislocker (1)
if it is BitLocker-protected, and mounts it read-only with
.BR nosuid , nodev ", and " noexec .
It prints the resulting mountpoint and leaves the volume mounted; run
.BR umount-windows (8)
to release it.
.PP
If an already-mounted filesystem is found to be the Windows system volume
(for example one listed in
.BR fstab (5)),
.B mount-windows
reports its existing mountpoint without creating a new mount of its own. Such
a volume is not tracked, and
.B umount-windows
will not act on it.
.PP
BitLocker volumes are opened with
.B dislocker
in read-only mode. Dislocker prompts for a recovery password without exposing
it in the process command line. TPM-only protectors generally require the
volume to be unlocked in Windows first.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --windows-device " DEVICE"
Mount a specific NTFS or BitLocker block device instead of searching all
local block devices.
.TP
.B --json
Print the result (or, with
.BR --list ,
the tracked mounts) as JSON.
.TP
.B --list
List the Windows volumes currently mounted by
.B mount-windows
instead of mounting a new one.
.TP
.B -v ", " --verbose
Print diagnostic detail (block devices considered, mount attempts) to
standard error. Repeat for more detail
.RB ( -vv " also prints a full Python traceback for unexpected"
environment failures; ordinary usage and validation errors are not
affected).
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /run/mount-windows
State tracking the mounts created by this tool, consulted by
.BR umount-windows (8).
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns zero on success and non-zero if no Windows volume was found, if
BitLocker unlocking failed, or if the caller is not root.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR umount-windows (8),
.BR qcom-ath-board-update (8),
.BR dislocker-fuse (1)
